Done. Published live at https://zefreak.github.io/city-council/ — the push was the publish.

**What the run found**

The main item is the Clark County comprehensive plan work session, **Wednesday 16 September 9am**. The Planning Commission's 3 September recommendation arrives internally inconsistent: item 11, consolidating five multifamily/office-residential zones in the unincorporated Vancouver UGA into three (R‑12→R‑24, R‑18/R‑22→R‑36, R‑30/R‑43→R‑50), **failed 3–4**, while items 63 and 65 — the Title 40 minimum and maximum density standards *for those same zones* — **passed 4–3**. The argument is over minimum densities; the development board's position, quoted in the transcript, is "all for increasing the maximums but keeping the minimums." I flagged the counter-argument from `context/` rather than overselling it: zoned capacity is not the binding constraint here, state gap financing is.

The enforceable farmland offset (1:1 permanent conservation, operational by 31 Dec 2027, urban holding designation) **failed 2–5, after every agricultural de-designation had already been approved**. Chair Johnson on the record about the last round: *"it wasn't staff, it was the Councilors said, no, they don't want that."*

The genuinely good item: the **Manufactured Home Park Overlay over 41 parcels passed 7–0**, credited on the record to residents who sat through a three-hour hearing.

**The DRA brief was rewritten, not amended.** The 57-page packet published after the 9 September run, so the earlier brief — written when only a one-page agenda existed — is superseded and says so. Degen & Degen, **$116,500** for two Hilton model rooms, with a whole-hotel Phase II priced nowhere; **$19,489,535** in escrow against $4,053,586 required; dedicated taxes at **180.7%** of a cap met on 12 May, the County PFD loan paid off 3 August. Clean SAO audit — said plainly. And at page 23 of the audit: in January 2026 the City **delayed transfers of bond-pledged lodging tax and PFD sales tax revenue**, released only after the DRA notified its bond trustee, then amended the DRA's bylaws in March to make the Executive Director a City at-will employee.

**Hearing dates, now firm:** 1 October 4:00pm, continued 2 October 10:00am, 5 October if needed, adoption 13 October — from the Community Planning Director on the record, driven by a Growth Management Hearings Board compliance deadline. I corrected the 15 September brief accordingly. Its agenda is byte-for-byte unchanged since 4 September.

**What I could not check:** the **DEAB letter** the Commission voted 5–2 to point Council at — not published anywhere, known only through conflicting characterisations in debate; the **draft drinking water ordinance** targeted for adoption 6 October; the total cost of the Hilton PIP; the subject and settlement terms of *Amadi vs. Clark County*; the City's side of the January 2026 DRA revenue dispute (this is written entirely from the DRA's own disclosure); and the Title 40 text behind most code items, including emergency shelters and permanent supportive housing at item 73, where I have the 7–0 vote but not the language.
